   "text": "2. What officials believed about Asian audiences, and how they judged their own programs (1950-1955)",
   "level": 1
  },
  {
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "Four documents from the first decade of the Cold War show the beliefs the strategies rested on: that Asians wait to see who is winning, that neutralism and race feeling are the openings Communist propaganda exploits, and that films, publications and English classes work even though their effect 'cannot be accurately measured'. Success is reported as activity (audiences, copies, classes), not as changed opinion."
  },
